  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A perfect early fall Saturday across the region as \nany lingering sprinkles to showers have move away from the area. \nQuiet conditions will continue into the evening with winds \neventually turning light and variable after 00Z. \n\nTONIGHT:  Temperatures will fall quickly this evening towards the \n40s before upper and then mid-lvl clouds increase after midnight. \nThe settling of a mid-lvl trough into the Dakotas will increase mid-\nlvl dPVA but also layered frontogenesis focused at the 850 mb level \nnear and south of the Missouri River and 700:600 mb frontogenesis \nnear or north of Highway 14.  CAMs and some lower resolution \nguidance struggling on the placement of this lift, leading to a \nhigher than typical spread in PoPs by time lift is maximized near or \nafter daybreak. High cloud bases will keep QPF light. \n\nSUNDAY: Morning light sprinkles/rain will persist at times through \nthe morning focused in or or two narrowing bands.  As the vorticity \npasses east late in the morning, we should see this activity \ndissipate, but leave a lingering mid-lvl cloud field in the area. \nTemperatures will recover slowly in the afternoon, but may struggle \nto reach the 70 degree mark.   \n\nSUNDAY NIGHT-MONDAY: Shortwave ridging moving through the Plains \nearly Sunday evening and early in the overnight hours will keep \nskies clear allowing for another quick temperature fall.  After \nmidnight hours, models remain in good agreement that troughing \narriving into the Western High Plains will induce a 40 knot LLJ \nthrough central South Dakota and Nebraska. Convergence on this LLJ \nwill spark scattered convection that may arrive into the Missouri \nRiver valley communities by 12Z. This non-severe convection \ncontinues to lift northeast Monday morning with most of the focus \nbefore 18Z. Given the orientation of the LLJ lingering and \npersistent showers may continue in the afternoon. My big concern on \nMonday may be with temperatures given ongoing cloud debris into\nthe afternoon. Latest NBM 25/75th percentile spread suggests \n7-12 degree temperature variance, with the 10/90th spread as \nhigh as 17 degrees. While we certainly could see highs in the \nmiddle to upper 70s, clouds could also hold us considerably \nlower. \n\nSevere weather risks Monday afternoon remain focused in only the \nvery far portions of the CWA where there may be potential for a warm \nfront to lift northward.  Greatest potential remains focused across \nnortheast Nebraska and northwest Iowa from 3pm to 7pm. It's also \nvery possible that continued convective debris really prevents any \nmeaning warming to arrive and limits the risk further south. \n\nTUESDAY-SATURDAY: We'll remain on the cool side of the baroclinic \nzone for the remainder of the work week. With mid-lvl flow remaining \nwesterly, this will present fewer opportunities for any large scale \nsurge of warm air northward.  Both limiting high temperatures to the \nupper 60s to lower 70s (near seasonal normals), but also keeping \nsevere weather risks minimal. Medium range ensembles hint at two \ntimeframes of focus for rainfall focused around Wednesday (lighter \namounts), and then late Thursday and Friday where stronger synoptic \nlift enters the Plains and forces more abundant low-lvl moisture \nnorthward. Ensembles have been trending the probabilities for >0.25\" \nhigher each model run and this trend may continue based on the 12Z \ndata.",
